 # 19\. Success Criteria

 The project will be considered successfully planned and implemented when:

 - The custom table is available.
- The required roles are created and assigned correctly.
- EEE, ECE, and CSE records exist.
- READ access is restricted according to the Branch requirement.
- Users without the required role cannot access protected records.
- Administrators can access all records.
- `bb2` controls creation.
- `bb3` controls modification.
- `bb4` controls deletion.
- Each CRUD operation can be independently tested.
- The observed results match the defined access-control matrix.

 ## Project Planning Summary

 The project will follow a **configuration → security implementation → testing → validation** approach:

```
Requirement Analysis
        ↓
Project Planning
        ↓
User & Role Setup
        ↓
Table & Field Setup
        ↓
Test Data
        ↓
READ ACL
        ↓
CREATE ACL
        ↓
WRITE ACL
        ↓
DELETE ACL
        ↓
Impersonation Testing
        ↓
Validation & Documentation
```

 The key planning principle is to **separate each CRUD permission into its own ACL while using the Branch field as the record-level security criterion for READ access**. This makes the implementation easier to test, troubleshoot, and demonstrate.
